UNIT isNumber
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG isNumber(num: any) -> boolean
DEPENDS-ON none

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BEHAVIOR  (ordered pipeline — exact + complete; transcribed from the source)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Source (verbatim semantics, is-number@7.0.0/index.js):
#
#   module.exports = function(num) {
#     if (typeof num === 'number') {
#       return num - num === 0;
#     }
#     if (typeof num === 'string' && num.trim() !== '') {
#       return Number.isFinite ? Number.isFinite(+num) : isFinite(+num);
#     }
#     return false;
#   };
#
# NODE 1 (BRANCH: typeof num === 'number')
#   When the argument is a primitive of type 'number' (this is the JS `typeof`
#   operator — boxed `new Number(x)` is typeof 'object', NOT 'number', and falls
#   through to NODE 3):
#     RETURN (num - num === 0)
#   Semantics of `num - num === 0`:
#     - For any FINITE number (incl. 0, -0, negatives, MAX_VALUE, subnormals):
#       num - num === 0  -> RETURN true.
#     - For NaN:        NaN - NaN === NaN, and NaN === 0 is false -> RETURN false.
#     - For +Infinity:  Infinity - Infinity === NaN -> RETURN false.
#     - For -Infinity:  (-Inf) - (-Inf) === NaN     -> RETURN false.
#     This is exactly "finite, non-NaN number". -0 returns true
#     (-0 - -0 === 0, and 0 === -0 is true under ===).
#     Note: a numeric LITERAL that overflows to Infinity (e.g. 1e309) is already
#     the primitive value Infinity at call time -> RETURN false.
#
# NODE 2 (BRANCH: typeof num === 'string' AND num.trim() !== '')
#   Reached only when NODE 1 was false (num is not a 'number' primitive).
#   GATE A: typeof num === 'string'  (primitive string; boxed `new String(..)`
#           is typeof 'object' -> fails this gate -> NODE 3).
#   GATE B: num.trim() !== ''  (uses String.prototype.trim, which strips the
#           ECMAScript WhiteSpace + LineTerminator set, INCLUDING U+FEFF BOM,
#           U+00A0 NBSP, U+1680, U+2000..U+200A, U+2028, U+2029, U+202F, U+205F,
#           U+3000 ideographic space, plus \t \n \v \f \r and ' ').
#           If the string is empty OR consists ENTIRELY of those whitespace
#           characters, trim() yields '' and GATE B FAILS -> fall to NODE 3
#           -> RETURN false.
#   If BOTH gates pass:
#     RETURN (Number.isFinite ? Number.isFinite(+num) : isFinite(+num))
#     - `+num` is the JS unary-plus / Number() string-to-number coercion. It:
#         * strips leading/trailing ECMAScript whitespace (same set as trim),
#         * accepts decimal: '5', '-5', '+5', '.5', '1.', '00.5', '1e3', '1E3',
#           '+.5', and surrounding-whitespace variants -> finite -> true,
#         * accepts the literal radix prefixes: hex '0x1'/'0X1F' (case-insensitive
#           x), binary '0b101'/'0B101', octal '0o17'/'0O17' -> finite -> true,
#         * accepts the strings 'Infinity'/'-Infinity'/'+Infinity' -> +num is
#           ±Infinity -> Number.isFinite is FALSE -> RETURN false,
#         * yields NaN for any malformed numeric string ('abc', '5px', '1,000',
#           '1.2.3', '1_000' [numeric separators NOT allowed by Number()],
#           '5n', '0xZZ', 'e3', '.', '-', non-ASCII digits like U+0660 / fullwidth
#           '５', 'NaN') -> Number.isFinite(NaN) is false -> RETURN false.
#     - Number.isFinite is present on all supported runtimes (node >=0.12), so the
#       global-isFinite fallback branch is effectively dead on modern Node; both
#       branches return the same boolean for the +num value here (since +num is
#       already a number, global isFinite and Number.isFinite agree). Use
#       Number.isFinite(+num) as the canonical computation.
#
# NODE 3 (DEFAULT)
#   RETURN false.
#   Reached for: typeof 'boolean' (true/false), 'undefined', null (typeof
#   'object'), 'bigint' (e.g. 5n -> typeof 'bigint', not 'number' or 'string'),
#   'symbol', 'function', and all 'object' values (plain objects {}, arrays []
#   and [5], RegExp, Date, boxed new Number(5), boxed new String('5'), etc.).
#   Also reached for an empty/whitespace-only string (GATE B failure) and for a
#   'string' whose +num is NaN or ±Infinity (NODE 2 computed false — same return).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VERIFIED TYPE ENVELOPE (each row run against the REAL package)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   RETURNS true:
#     - number primitive that is finite & non-NaN: 5, 0, -0, -3.5, 1.2,
#       Number.MAX_VALUE  (and any finite double)
#     - numeric string (trim non-empty AND +num finite):
#         '5' '1.2' '-5' '+5' '.5' '1.' '00.5' '1e3' '0x1' '0X1F' '0b101' '0o17'
#         '-0' '0.0' '0x0' '  5  ' (surrounding ASCII/Unicode whitespace, incl.
#         NBSP/\f/\v/\r/\t pads)
#   RETURNS false:
#     - number primitive NaN, Infinity, -Infinity (and any literal overflowing
#       to Infinity at call time, e.g. 1e309)
#     - string '' (empty), '   ' (whitespace-only, incl. BOM-only '﻿',
#       NBSP-only, ideographic-space-only, '\t\n')
#     - non-numeric string: 'abc' 'Infinity' '-Infinity' 'NaN' '5px' '1,000'
#       '1.2.3' '1_000' '5n' '0xZZ' 'e3' '.' '-' '٠'(U+0660) '５'(fullwidth)
#     - null, undefined
#     - boolean true, boolean false
#     - bigint (e.g. 5n)
#     - plain object {}, array [], array [5]
#     - boxed new Number(5), boxed new String('5')
#     - RegExp /5/, function, Symbol
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ORACLE value -> oracle.json
#   Deterministic, pure, side-effect-free. No clock/RNG/IO/net seam.
#   Oracle is the (input -> boolean) value table; the harness computes expecteds
#   by running the real fn. genInputs supplies the stratified inputs.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SCOPE
#   IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly): the entire three-node pipeline above.
#     The total contract is captured by the source's three branches; the unit
#     leans on host JS primitives (typeof, the `-` operator's IEEE-754 NaN
#     semantics, String.prototype.trim's whitespace set, and the Number()/unary-+
#     string-to-number grammar). A blind re-emitter MUST reproduce these via the
#     target's equivalent built-ins (NOT a hand-rolled numeric parser) so the
#     whitespace set and radix-prefix grammar match exactly.
#   CARRIED DATA: none (no literal tables).
#   OUT-OF-CONTRACT: none (no stateful mutators, no side APIs).
#   OUT-OF-ENVELOPE (documented, NOT emitted by genInputs because they do not
#     survive the oracle JSON codec round-trip): live functions, Symbol values,
#     and identity-bearing boxed primitives (new Number/new String) where the
#     wrapper identity matters. Their behavior is nonetheless fully specified:
#       function   -> NODE 3 -> false
#       Symbol     -> NODE 3 -> false
#       new Number(5), new String('5') -> typeof 'object' -> NODE 3 -> false
#     (genInputs DOES emit codec-faithful members of the false set: null,
#      undefined, NaN, ±Infinity, booleans, {}, [], bigint — see the generator.)
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
PROVENANCE is-number@7.0.0  (jonschlinkert) — repo github.com/jonschlinkert/is-number,
